# KSV-0014: the web Deployment's `seed-html` init container runs with a
# writable root filesystem on purpose — its only job is `cp -a`ing the image's
# baked static assets into the emptyDir the main `web` container then mounts
# read-write (see templates/web-deployment.yaml's header comment). The main
# container itself keeps readOnlyRootFilesystem: true; only this scratch
# init container is exempted.
KSV-0014

# KSV-01010: "ConfigMap stores sensitive contents in key(s) `access_key`,
# `secret_key`". It reads the key NAMES; both values render as `""` on purpose.
# Every credential in this chart is an `existingSecret` reference delivered as
# WNK_-prefixed environment (server-deployment.yaml), which is precisely so the
# ConfigMap holds none — the finding fires on the shape of a template that exists
# to be empty. Removing the keys is not an option: the config schema requires them
# to be present for the env override to have something to override.
KSV-01010

# KSV-0125: "uses an image from an untrusted registry". The registry is ghcr.io,
# which is where this project publishes (release.yaml). Trivy's default trusted
# list is Docker Hub and a handful of clouds; the alternative to ignoring it is
# maintaining a trusted-registry list that says "ours is ours".
KSV-0125

# KSV-0011: "should set resources.limits.cpu". Deliberately absent, and the one
# finding here that is a considered disagreement rather than a false positive: a
# CPU limit throttles under burst instead of shedding, which for a latency-
# sensitive API is worse than the noisy-neighbour problem it solves. What actually
# bounds a runaway pod is the memory limit, and `server.resources.limits.memory`
# sets one. Revisit if this ever runs somewhere CPU quota is contractual.
KSV-0011
